Upstream CVE in Crypt::Age (CPANSec, reported 2026-09-04, publication planned
for 2026-09-18). The fix is tracked there as k45; File::SOPS has k192.

PROBLEM
Crypt::Age did not bound the stanza count in an age header. Every stanza costs
an X25519 scalar multiplication before the header can be authenticated.
Measured on 0.003: 4000 stanzas = 15.7 s of CPU, then rejection.

RELEVANCE HERE -- not yet assessed, and that is the actual task
OCP/Keys.pm and OCP/Secrets.pm call Crypt::Age->decrypt. The DoS only reaches
paths that decrypt ciphertext somebody else wrote. For self-generated key
layers the risk is small. To settle: whether any path decrypts third-party
input (secrets from a cluster, from a repository, from an API response).

TO DO
1. Walk the decrypt calls and answer who writes the ciphertext in each case.
2. Raise the cpanfile pin to Crypt::Age 0.004, which carries the cap.
3. If any path takes third-party input: Crypt::Age gained max_stanzas
   (default 128) -- set it deliberately there rather than inheriting the
   default.

No edits from p5-crypt-age; this ticket is the handover.

2026-09-09 06:27 Audit done: OCP has exactly two Crypt::Age decrypt calls (OCP::Keys, self-generated single-recipient key material); neither takes third-party input. The real third-party decrypt surface is inside File::SOPS (its own board). cpanfile pin raised from 0.003 to Crypt::Age 0.004, which carries the CVE-2026-85783 recipient-stanza cap. Default cap 128 is safe for OCP so no explicit max_stanzas set (not speculative). No Changes entry (never-released dist). make test-host green: 81 files, 1292 tests. Commit 6cc4a79. Note: 0.004 is pinned ahead of its CPAN release, so the binding Docker make test waits on it, same class as the 1.108 siblings in k133.
